  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 240 PM CST Fri Nov 6 2026\n\nCurrently, embedded S/WV moving east across far south central\nCanada and the Northern Plains, with a weak sfc boundary across\nthe Dakotas. Lingering clouds associated with the wave have kept\nafternoon temperatures on the cooler side south central and \neast in the 40s, while we have warmed up elsewhere into the 50s\nwhere the sky is clear.\n\nRAP, HRRR, and RRFS all suggest fog redeveloping south central\nand southeast this evening ahead of the quasi-stationary\nboundary, then eroding west to east 06-14Z as the boundary \nshifts east. Have this in the forecast and HWO. Behind this\nboundary, winds become breezy out of the west/northwest on\nSaturday, with the combination of sunshine and mixing warming\ntemperatures during the day well into the 50s to lower 60s. \n\nUpper ridging over the Desert Southwest expands north and \nnortheast across the Rockies and into the Northern Plains\nSunday, with our flow aloft becoming more zonal and helping to\nmaintain milder air over the region, with highs Sunday again in\nthe mid 50s to lower 60s.\n\nAbove normal temperatures and mainly dry conditions look to\npersist well into next week. Ridging to our west and overall\ntroughing across the eastern CONUS may allow weak impulses at \ntimes moving across the region. Uncertainty very high with any\nprecipitation, which the low POP numbers of the NMB reflect.\nNear to above normal high temperatures in the mid 40s to mid \n50s are forecast, cooler to the east and warmer to the west, \nwith low 60s possible west on the warm side of the flow \npossible.",
